# Session 2 lab, part B: learn from one worked query, then write two.
#
# Read Y1 slowly before doing anything. It is a complete example: it counts
# orders per plant. Y2 and Y3 use the same pattern of (1) start with the
# facts, (2) add the condition, and (3) choose what the answer should show.
#
# Write Y2 and Y3 under their markers, replacing the TODO line. Then run:
#     python check_my_queries.py            (Oxigraph, inside Python)
#     python check_my_queries.py fuseki     (against your loaded Fuseki)
# The checker tells you whether the worked example and your two answers are right.
# Only open solutions/my_queries_solutions.sparql after you have tried.
#
# Every query needs this line at its top:
#     PREFIX ul: <https://ul.edu.lb/kr/scm#>
# Useful predicates: ul:fromPlant, ul:shipsFrom, ul:orderedBy, ul:servesPort.

# --- Y1 · worked example: GROUP BY counts orders per plant ---
# Question: how many orders does each plant ship? Show ?plant and ?orders,
# largest first.
#
# 1. Start with the fact in every order row: an order has a from-plant.
#        ?order ul:fromPlant ?plant
# 2. COUNT counts the orders that share each value of ?plant.
# 3. GROUP BY makes one result row per plant.
# 4. DESC puts the largest count first.
#
# Expect: PLANT03 is first with 8,541 orders. Run the checker now if you want
# to see the result before trying Y2.
PREFIX ul: <https://ul.edu.lb/kr/scm#>
SELECT ?plant (COUNT(?order) AS ?orders) WHERE {
  ?order ul:fromPlant ?plant .
} GROUP BY ?plant ORDER BY DESC(?orders)

